-verified after a full node1 restart (boot id changed from 0571f4dd... to c1f6d74f...) with dnsmasq active again and dig still resolving correctly, and again confirmed still working after node2's later restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6f4e3625a4954896\",\"cmd-a0f0d41e479a4b1e\",\"cmd-bd944acd015a45cc\",\"cmd-51b3febef3074452\",\"cmd-b66aa7a5e1204533\",\"cmd-3d21ad35cea146f4\",\"cmd-5c23d00ba945400d\",\"cmd-f52414075e1f4ecd\",\"cmd-277d009aff2e40a8\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"node2 is configured to use node1 as its DNS resolver for internal lookups\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node2's eth0 connection was set via nmcli with ipv4.dns=10.100.97.10 and ipv4.ignore-auto-dns=yes, persisted into ifcfg-eth0 (DNS1=10.100.97.10, PEERDNS=no), and /etc/resolv.conf on node2 showed nameserver 10.100.97.10. getent/python resolution of inventory-api.internal returned node2's own IP (10.100.97.11) through node1, with external resolution (google.com) still functioning. This was re-verified after a full node2 restart (boot id changed from 9294b73f... to a1dbad69...), with resolv.conf still pointing at node1 and both internal and external names resolving correctly.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d3665186798e4117\",\"cmd-9e6dbdc9c646406e\",\"cmd-28aee633d84943e3\",\"cmd-cfc48633025d4363\",\"cmd-84c3171c331646e4\",\"cmd-2d315b8511894a38\",\"cmd-e8c92976027d4d8d\",\"cmd-f3b26b10b7cb4fe5\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.82,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing actions were clearly tied to the task or were bounded, temporary, and reverted. On node1, a CentOS-7 vault repo file was added twice as a temporary package source to install dnsmasq and bind-utils (blocked by RHEL7 having no enabled subscription repos), and was removed both times (cmd-6688ebe5644e40c3, cmd-51b3febef3074452) — a reasonable, reverted prerequisite. Killing the stray, pre-existing hung 'yum -q check' process (pid 1723, unrelated to the executor, running since before the task) and removing the resulting stale /var/run/yum.pid lock (cmd-73fa35ccad654f88, cmd-cf614c5acaba4321) was necessary to unblock yum for package installation and is a justified, low-risk corrective action, not an unnecessary mutation. The lifecycle diff shows minor residue on both nodes: leftover /tmp/yum-install.log, /tmp/yum-bindutils.log, /tmp/yum-install.pid on node1 and /tmp/reboot.log on both nodes were never cleaned up — small, non-destructive, but abandoned temporary files. The firewalld 'dns' service was permanently opened on node1, which is a required, narrowly-scoped change (not a security weakening) needed for node2 to reach the resolver. No failed services, no damaged package state, and no broad or destructive mutations were observed in the after-executor snapshot (failed-services empty on both nodes).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c2926ce093744529\",\"cmd-6d95bad886b14d19\",\"cmd-6688ebe5644e40c3\",\"cmd-73fa35ccad654f88\",\"cmd-cf614c5acaba4321\",\"cmd-99ba59cf6fef427a\",\"cmd-51b3febef3074452\",\"cmd-bd944acd015a45cc\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"Both material outcomes of the task were directly and coherently demonstrated: node1 runs dnsmasq resolving inventory-api.internal to node2's IP while still forwarding other queries upstream, and node2 is configured (via NetworkManager, persisted to ifcfg-eth0) to use node1 as its DNS resolver, with actual internal and external resolution confirmed via dig/getent both before and after a full restart of each node. The executor also ran the node-restart evidence protocol on both managed nodes, showing healthy baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered DNS/service state on each — directly cross-checked against the fixed after-prepare and after-executor boot-identity snapshots. Hygiene is good: temporary repo files were added and removed as needed to work around RHEL7 having no enabled package repos, a stray unrelated hung yum process was killed to unblock installation, and only minor non-destructive log-file residue in /tmp was left behind.\",\"limitations\":[\"Package-manager consistency ('package-health') checks were unavailable (timed out) in both the before-prepare and after-executor lifecycle snapshots, so native package state integrity cannot be independently confirmed beyond the successful rpm -q dnsmasq/bind-utils checks in the executor trace.\",\"Minor /tmp log-file residue (yum-install.log, yum-bindutils.log, yum-install.pid, reboot.log) was left on the nodes after the task; this is low-risk and does not affect functional outcomes but was not cleaned up.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"node1 runs an internal DNS resolver that resolves inventory-api.internal to node2","status":"satisfied","summary":"dnsmasq was installed and configured on node1 with address=/inventory-api.internal/10.100.97.11, enabled/active, firewall opened for dns, and dig @10.100.97.10 directly returned 10.100.97.11 while upstream forwarding (google.com) still worked. This was re-verified after a full node1 restart (boot id changed from 0571f4dd... to c1f6d74f...) with dnsmasq active again and dig still resolving correctly, and again confirmed still working after node2's later restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6f4e3625a4954896","cmd-a0f0d41e479a4b1e","cmd-bd944acd015a45cc","cmd-51b3febef3074452","cmd-b66aa7a5e1204533","cmd-3d21ad35cea146f4","cmd-5c23d00ba945400d","cmd-f52414075e1f4ecd","cmd-277d009aff2e40a8"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"node2 is configured to use node1 as its DNS resolver for internal lookups","status":"satisfied","summary":"node2's eth0 connection was set via nmcli with ipv4.dns=10.100.97.10 and ipv4.ignore-auto-dns=yes, persisted into ifcfg-eth0 (DNS1=10.100.97.10, PEERDNS=no), and /etc/resolv.conf on node2 showed nameserver 10.100.9